<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-XXX-activity">

	<title>Greatest Common Divisors, the Euclidean Algorithm, and Linear Combinations</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Numbers</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Greatest Common Divisors</title>
		
    <p>Here's the formal definition of greatest common divisor:</p>
    
    <definition>
  	<statement>
    	<p>
      Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers, not both zero.
      A <term>common divisor</term> of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> is any integer <m>k</m>
      such that <m>k\mid a</m> and <m>k\mid b</m>.
      We say that an integer <m>d</m> is the <term>greatest common divisor</term> of
      <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, denoted <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>, provided that <m>d</m> is
      a common divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, and if <m>k</m> is a common
      divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> then <m>k\leq d</m>.
    	</p>
  	</statement>
	</definition>
    </paragraphs>
    
    <paragraphs>
    	<title>Applying The Euclidean Algorithm</title>

  		<p>
   		 Find <m>\gcd(27182, 3141)</m> using the Euclidean algorithm.
    		If you'd like another example see page 26 of your text.
  		</p>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Why The Euclidean Algorithm Works</title>

  <p>
    Here's a theorem that explains why the Euclidean algorithm works:
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers, not both <m>0</m> and suppose
        that
        <md>
          b = aq + r
        </md>
        for some integers <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>.
        Then <m>\gcd(b,a) = \gcd(a,r)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <!-- Let $d = \gcd(b,a)$. To prove this theorem we need to show two things: -->
  <!--	\begin{itemize}-->
  <!--	\item The integer $d$ is a common divisor of $a$ and $r$.-->
  <!--	\item If $k$ is a common divisor of $a$ and $r$ then $k\leq d$.-->
  <!--	\end{itemize}-->
  		<exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          Assuming the hypothesis of the theorem, prove that if <m>d</m> is a
          common divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> then <m>d</m> is a common
          divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>.
        </p>
		</exercise>

      	<exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          Assuming the hypothesis of the theorem, prove that if <m>d</m> is a
          common divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>r</m> then <m>d</m> is a common
          divisor of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
        </p>
     	</exercise>

      	<exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          How do the previous 2 parts show that <m>\gcd(a,r) = \gcd(a,b)</m>?
        </p>
      	</exercise>

      	<exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          How is this theorem related to the Euclidean algorithm?
        </p>
      	</exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	  <title>Linear Combinations and Back Substitution</title>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.
        A <term>linear combination</term> of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> is an integer
        that can be written as <m>ax+by</m> for some integers <m>x</m> and
        <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
        <p>
          Recall that we found <m>\gcd(540,765) = 45</m> using the Euclidean
          algorithm as follows:
        </p>
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="eu-alg-1">765&amp;= 540\cdot 1 + 225</mrow>
          <mrow xml:id="eu-alg-2">540&amp;= 225 \cdot 2 + 90</mrow>
          <mrow xml:id="eu-alg-3">225&amp;= 90 \cdot 2 + 45</mrow>
          <mrow xml:id="eu-alg-4">90&amp;= 45 \cdot 2 + 0.</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>Using these equations, we will find integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>765 x + 540 y = 45</m>.</p>

		<exercise workspace="2in">
              <p>
                Note from equation <xref ref="eu-alg-3" /> we have
                <md>
                  45 = 225+90\cdot (-2).
                </md>
                Solve for <m>90</m> in equation <xref ref="eu-alg-2" /> and substitute into this
                equation.
                Do not simplify.
              </p>
           </exercise>

          <exercise workspace="2in">
              <p>
                Rewrite the equation you found in (a) so that you have 45 as a
                linear combination of <m>225</m> and <m>540</m>.
              </p>
          </exercise>

          <exercise workspace="2in">
              <p>
                Now, using equation <xref ref="eu-alg-1" />, substitute for <m>225</m> in the
                equation you have for part (b).
                Rewrite so that you have <m>45</m> as a linear combination of
                <m>765</m> and <m>540</m>.
              </p>
           </exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
   
   <exercise workspace="3in">
              <p>
                Using the Euclidean algorithm you can always find
                <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>.
                Then using this <q>back-substitution</q> method with the Euclidean
                algorithm you can always write the greatest common divisor of
                <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> as a linear combination of <m>a</m> and
                <m>b</m>.
                Together some call this the <q>Extended Euclidean Algorithm</q>.
                Using the Euclidean Algorithm find <m>\gcd(816, 188)</m> and
                find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
                <m>\gcd(816,188) = 816x + 188y</m>.
              </p>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="3in">
              <p>
                <em>If time:</em> Are these linear combinations unique?
                E.g., can you find more than one linear combination of <m>3</m>
                and <m>5</m> that equals <m>1</m>?
              </p>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="3in">
              <p>
                <em>If time:</em> Use your work finding <m>\gcd(27182,3141)</m>
                to write <m>1</m> as a linear combination of <m>27182</m> and
                <m>3141</m>.
              </p>
            </exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Bezout's Identity</title>

  <p>
    Here are two important theorems in number theory:
  </p>

  <theorem>
  <title>Bezout's Identity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers, not both
        zero.
        Then <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> can be written as a linear combination of <m>a</m>
        and <m>b</m>.
        That is, there exist integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
        <md>
          \gcd(a,b) = ax + by.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    This is at first surprising!
    There is no reason there should be integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
    <m>12345x+54321y = 3</m>.
    But based on the work on the previous page, you can always back-substitute
    from the Euclidean algorithm.
  </p>

  <corollary>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers, not both zero.
        Then <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> is equal to the smallest positive linear
        combination of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </corollary>

  <p>
    The fact that it is the smallest positive linear combination is a Weekly
    Practice Problem - <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> divides every linear combination of
    <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will skip proofs of these theorems and opt to see applications of these
    theorems instead.
  </p>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <p>
          Suppose <m>a=3</m> and <m>b=2</m>.
          Then, letting <m>x = 1</m> and <m>y=1</m> we find <m>ax+by=5</m>.
          Does it follow from Bezout's Identity that <m>\gcd(3,2)=5</m>?
        </p>
      </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <p>
          Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.
          Suppose there exist integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
          <m>ax+by=k</m>.
          Does it follow that <m>\gcd(a,b)=k</m>?
          If so, explain.
          If not, what can you say about <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>?
          
          Hint: Show <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> divides any linear combination of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>

   <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <p>
          Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.
          Suppose there exist integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
          <m>ax+by=1</m>.
          Does it follow that <m>\gcd(a,b)=1</m>?
          If so, explain.
          If not, what can you say about <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>?
          Note: For two integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, not both <m>0</m>, we
          say <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are <em>relatively prime</em> provided that
          <m>\gcd(a,b) = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </exercise>  
    </paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Fun With Linear Combinations</title>

		<exercise>
		<introduction>
        <p>
          Let's see a connection between Multiplicative Inverses and GCD's:
         </p>
         </introduction>
         
         <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                In <m>\mathbb{Z}_{10}</m>, find <m>([7]_{10})^{-1}</m>.
                That is, find the multiplicative inverse of <m>[7]</m> in
                <m>\mathbb{Z}_{10}</m>.
              </p>
            </task>

          <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                Find a linear combination of <m>7</m> and <m>10</m> that is
                equal to <m>\gcd(7,10)</m>.
                (If you don't see it right away, use the Euclidean Algorithm and
                back substitution, it's good practice!) How is your answer
                related to the multiplicative inverse of <m>[7]</m> in
                <m>\mathbb{Z}_{10}</m>?
              </p>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                Does <m>[2]_{10}</m> have a multiplicative inverse in
                <m>\mathbb{Z}_{10}</m>?
                How does this relate to greatest common divisors and linear
                combinations?
              </p>
            </task>
		</exercise>

      <exercise>
      	<introduction>
        <p>
          In Die Hard 2, there's a scene where a bomb will go off unless they
          get exactly 4 gallons of water in a jug. In the movie, they are given a 5 gallon jug and a 3 gallon jug
                and a fountain with unlimited water.</p>
         </introduction>
         
          <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                How can you get 4 gallons of water in a jug in this situation?
              </p>
            </task>

           <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                Note that <m>\gcd(5,3)=1</m>.
                Write <m>1 = 5x+3y</m> for some integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
                How does this give you instructions for how to get 1 gallon of
                water in one of the jugs?
                (If time: Is there a unique solution?)
              </p>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
              <p>
                In Die Hard 25, they are given a <m>6</m> gallon jug and a
                <m>15</m> gallon jug, and asked to get <m>4</m> gallons of water
                in a jug.
                Is this possible?
                Why or why not?
              </p>
            </task>
		</exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>
	</page>
	
<page xml:id="sec-gcds-euclid-review">
<paragraphs>
	<title>Review questions</title>
    
    Make sure you can answer the following questions:
    <ul>
    <li>
    	<p>What is the formal definition of the greatest common divisor of two integers, that is <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>?</p>
 	</li>
 
 	<li>
		<p>What is the Euclidean algorithm, how do we apply it, and why does it work?</p>
	</li>
	
	<li>
		<p>How can we use the Euclidean algorithm and "back-substitution" to write <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> as a linear combination of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>?</p>
	</li>
	
	<li>
		<p>What does Bezout's identity say? What are some erroneous applications of Bezout's identity?</p>
	</li>
	</ul>
      
	<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>
</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 4</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<introduction><p>
		  (This is good practice for Learning Target 6.) For each of the following values of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, use the
          Euclidean algorithm to determine <m>\gcd(a,b)</m>.
          Then find integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
          <m>ax+by = \gcd(a,b)</m>.
          </p></introduction>
          
          <task>
              <p>
                <m>a= 525</m>, <m>b=252</m>
              </p>
           </task>

           <task>
              <p>
                <m>a=54321</m>, <m>b=12345</m>
              </p>
           </task>

           <task>
              <p>
                <m>a=27182</m>, <m>b=-3141</m>.
                (Note we did <m>\gcd(27182,3141)</m> in class!)
              </p>
           </task>
    	</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<introduction><p>(This is good practice for Learning Target 7.) Decide whether each of the following statements is true or false.
          For those that are true, explain why.
          For those that are false, give a counterexample and then change
          <em>one word or symbol</em> in the statement to make it true.
          For each statement, assume that <m>a,b,</m> and <m>d</m> are positive
          integers.</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>If <m>ax+by = 1</m> for some integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>,
                then <m>\gcd(a,b)=1</m>.</p></task>
            <task><p>If <m>ax+by \neq 1</m> for some integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>,
                then <m>\gcd(a,b)\neq 1</m>.</p></task>
            <task><p>If <m>x+by=d</m> for some integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then
                <m>\gcd(a,b) = d</m>.</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<statement><p>
          Prove that <m>\gcd(a,b)</m> divides every linear combination of
          <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
          That is, prove that for all <m>x,y\in\mathbb{Z}</m>,
          <m>\gcd(a,b) \mid ax+by</m>.
          Explain why this proves the Corollary to Bezout's Identity given in
          this packet.
        </p></statement>
		
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>

